### Convergence Issues and Outliers


```{r}
#| label: convergence-outliers-second-sim-study
#| echo: false
#| output: false

get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2 <- function(method, dist, n, rel, model = "Full",
                                                  equation = c("eta4", "eta5"),
                                                  rate_type = c("convergence", "outlier", "final")) {
  equation <- match.arg(equation)
  rate_type <- match.arg(rate_type)
  
  col_map <- c(
    "convergence" = "Convergence_Rate_This_Method",
    "outlier" = "Outlier_Rate_This_Method", 
    "final" = "Percent_Total_Excluded"
  )
  
  col_name <- col_map[rate_type]
  
  # needs representative parameter for each equation
  # for eta4: use "eta1" (first predictor)
  # for eta5: use "eta4" (first predictor)
  param_to_use <- ifelse(equation == "eta4", "eta1", "eta4")
  
  result <- results_study_2 %>%
    filter(
      Method == toupper(method),
      Distribution == dist,
      SampleSize == n,
      Reliability == rel,
      Model == model,
      Equation == equation,
      Parameter == param_to_use
    ) %>%
    pull(!!sym(col_name))
  
  if(length(result) > 0) result[1] else NA
}

# worst outlier rates across both equations
#results_study_2$results %>%
#  filter((Equation == "eta4" & Parameter == "eta1") | 
#         (Equation == "eta5" & Parameter == "eta4")) %>%
#  group_by(Method, Equation) %>%
#  arrange(desc(Outlier_Rate_This_Method)) %>%
#  slice_head(n = 2) %>%
#  select(Method, Equation, Model, Distribution, SampleSize, Reliability, Outlier_Rate_This_Method) %>%
#  arrange(Method, Equation, desc(Outlier_Rate_This_Method))

# Worst convergence rates for each method and equation
#results_study_2$results %>%
#  filter((Equation == "eta4" & Parameter == "eta1") | 
#         (Equation == "eta5" & Parameter == "eta4")) %>%
#  group_by(Method, Equation) %>%
#  arrange(Convergence_Rate_This_Method) %>%
#  slice_head(n = 2) %>%
#  select(Method, Equation, Model, Distribution, SampleSize, Reliability, Convergence_Rate_This_Method) %>%
#  arrange(Method, Equation, Convergence_Rate_This_Method)
```


Convergence problems were most severe for UPI, particularly with a moderate sample, low reliability, and uniform predictors. Rates fell to `r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("UPI", "uniform", 400, 0.4, "Full", "eta4", "convergence"), 2)`% for both full model and linear model. QML converged more often under these conditions but still at suboptimal levels (`r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("QML", "uniform", 400, 0.4, "Full", "eta4", "convergence"), 2)`% with the full model; `r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("QML", "uniform", 400, 0.4, "Linear", "eta4", "convergence"), 2)`% with the linear model). LSAM converged `r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("LSAM", "normal", 400, 0.4, "Full", "eta4", "convergence"), 2)`% under all conditions. For normal and right-skewed predictors, all approaches had a convergence rate above 98%, with a few exceptions.

Outlier rates also varied substantially across methods. UPI again performed worst under the condition of uniform predictors with moderate sample size and low reliability, reaching `r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("UPI", "uniform", 400, 0.4, "Linear", "eta4", "outlier"), 2)`% with the linear model and `r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("UPI", "uniform", 400, 0.4, "Full", "eta4", "outlier"), 2)`% with the full model. QML showed much lower outlier rates overall, with the highest occurring under right-skewed predictors (`r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("QML", "nonnormal", 400, 0.4, "Linear", "eta4", "outlier"), 2)`% with the linear model), followed by uniform predictors (`r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("QML", "uniform", 400, 0.4, "Full", "eta4", "outlier"), 2)`% with the full model). LSAM’s highest outlier rates also occurred outside normality, with uniform predictors in the full model (`r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("LSAM", "uniform", 400, 0.4, "Full", "eta4", "outlier"), 2)`%) and right-skewed predictors in the linear model (`r round(get_convergence_outlier_rate_study_2("LSAM", "nonnormal", 400, 0.4, "Linear", "eta4", "outlier"), 2)`%). We note the final sample size for the condition with low reliability and uniform distribution is relatively small (see Table C2 in Appendix C for more information).  


```{r}
#| label: plots-second-sim-study
#| echo: false

study_2_normal_data <- prepare_study2_data(results_study_2, "normal")
study_2_nonnormal_data <- prepare_study2_data(results_study_2, "nonnormal")
study_2_uniform_data <- prepare_study2_data(results_study_2, "uniform")

# RELATIVE BIAS (full model, N=400, all Distributions) 
dat_relative_bias_400_full_all_study_2 <- bind_rows(
  study_2_normal_data$bias_relative %>%
    filter(Model == "Full", SampleSize == "400"),
  study_2_nonnormal_data$bias_relative %>%
    filter(Model == "Full", SampleSize == "400"),
  study_2_uniform_data$bias_relative %>%
    filter(Model == "Full", SampleSize == "400")
) %>%
  droplevels()

p_relative_bias_400_full_all_study_2 <- plot_bias(
  dat_relative_bias_400_full_all_study_2,
  shapes = SHAPES_3,
  ltys = LTYS_3,
  facet_formula = Parameter ~ Distribution,
  y_breaks = seq(-1, 1.5, by = 0.5),
  y_limits = c(-1, 1.5),
