# Local Standard Errors for LSAM 

To obtain two-step corrected standard errors for the parameters of the structural part of the model ($\boldsymbol{\theta}_2$), we use the familiar (sandwich) formula for robust standard errors [@savalei_rosseel_2021]:
$$
\mathrm{Var}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_2) = \frac{1}{N}\left[(\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{\top}\mathcal{I}_1\boldsymbol{\Delta})^{-1}(\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{\top}\mathcal{I}_1\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\eta}\mathcal{I}_1\boldsymbol{\Delta})(\boldsymbol{\Delta}^{\top}\mathcal{I}_1\boldsymbol{\Delta})^{-1}\right]
$$

\noindent where $\mathcal{I}_1$ is the unit expected information matrix of the unrestricted second step model (h1), $\boldsymbol{\Delta}$ is the Jacobian of the function that maps $\boldsymbol{\theta}_2$ to $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_2)$, and $N$ is the number of observations. The critical ingredient is $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\eta}$, which represents $N$ times the asymptotic (co)variance matrix of the sufficient statistics that enter the second step. The sufficient statistics are (a subset of) the mean vector $\mathbb{E}(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star})$ and the non-redundant elements of the variance-covariance matrix $\mathrm{Var}(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star})$. We collect these elements in the column vector $L(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star})$:
$$
L(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}) = \big[\mathbb{E}(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}),\,\mathrm{vech}(\mathrm{Var}(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}))\big]^{\top}.
$$

\noindent Let $l_i(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star})$ denote the casewise contribution to $L(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star})$ so that we can write
$$
L(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}^{N}l_i(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}).
$$

\noindent Then, if we would ignore the two-step process, a naive expression for $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\eta}$ would be
$$
\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\eta}^{(naive)} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}^{N}l_i^{(c)}(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star})\,l_i^{(c)}(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star}\otimes\boldsymbol{\eta}^{\star})^{\top}
$$

\noindent where $l_i^{(c)}$ is the centered version of $l_i$; that is $l_i^{(c)} = l_i - L$. To obtain an estimate of $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\eta}$ that takes into account the two-stage estimation procedure, we follow a procedure similar to @wall_amemiya_2000. Assuming the measurement errors are normally distributed, $\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\eta}$ can then be expressed as follows:
$$
\boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\eta} = \boldsymbol{\Gamma}_{\eta}^{(naive)} + N\cdot\mathbf{C}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{11}\mathbf{C}^{\top}
$$

\noindent where $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{11}$ is the variance-covariance matrix of the step one parameters ($\boldsymbol{\theta}_1$), and the matrix $\mathbf{C}$ can be constructed as
$$
\mathbf{C} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}^{N}\frac{\partial l_i(\boldsymbol{\theta}_1)}{\partial\boldsymbol{\theta}_1}
$$

\noindent where we now consider $l_i(\boldsymbol{\theta}_1)$ to be a function of the step one parameters ($\boldsymbol{\theta}_1$).



{{< pagebreak >}}



# Data Generation for Simulation Studies

Population models were first calibrated under full normality to achieve the desired reliabilities ($\rho \approx 0.4, 0.6, 0.8$) and $R^2 \approx 0.30$. The resulting structural residual variances ($\psi$) and measurement error variances ($\theta_{jj}$) were then held fixed across all distributional conditions. Using the fixed variance parameters from calibration, data were generated in three stages: (i) correlated exogenous latent predictors, (ii) dependent latent variables through the structural model, and (iii) observed indicators through the measurement model.

**Stage 1: Exogenous Predictors.** Correlated predictor latent variables were generated using the VITA method as implemented in the *covsim* package (version `r covsim_version`) [@gronneberg_foldnes_2022] with sampling via *rvinecopulib* (version `r vinecop`) [@R-rvinecopulib]. Predictor latent variables were specified to follow covariance structures:
$$
\boldsymbol{\Phi}_1 =
\begin{bmatrix} 
1 & 0.375 \\ 
0.375 & 1 
\end{bmatrix}
\quad \text{(Simulation 1)}, \qquad
\boldsymbol{\Phi}_2 = 
\begin{bmatrix} 
1 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\ 
0.3 & 1 & 0.3 \\ 
0.2 & 0.3 & 1 
\end{bmatrix}
\quad \text{(Simulation 2)}.
$$

Under the normal condition, predictors had Gaussian marginals with mean zero and the specified covariance structure. Under the right-skewed condition, gamma marginals with shape $= 1$ and rate $= 1$ were employed, producing natural skewness $\gamma_1 \approx 2$ and excess kurtosis $\gamma_2 \approx 6$. Gamma-distributed variables were shifted to have population mean zero by subtracting their natural mean ($\text{shape}/\text{rate} = 1$), ensuring comparability across distributional conditions. Under the uniform condition, predictors were generated from symmetric uniform distributions $U[-\sqrt{3\phi_{kk}}, \sqrt{3\phi_{kk}}]$, which naturally have mean zero and variance $\phi_{kk}$ (the diagonal element of $\boldsymbol{\Phi}$). Thus, all exogenous predictors were centered at zero across all distributional conditions, differing only in their shape characteristics. In all conditions, VITA constructed a vine copula to impose the specified dependence structure while preserving the marginal distributions.

**Stage 2: Structural Model.** Dependent latent variables were generated according to the structural equations with fixed residual variances $\psi$ (determined during calibration to yield $R^2 \approx 0.30$):
$$
R^2_\eta = \frac{\operatorname{Var}(\eta^*)}{\operatorname{Var}(\eta^*) + \psi} \approx 0.30,
$$

\noindent where $\eta^*$ denotes the deterministic portion of the dependent latent variable. Structural disturbances were sampled independently from $\mathcal{N}(0,\psi)$.

**Stage 3: Measurement Model.** Observed indicators were generated as
$$
\mathbf{y} = \boldsymbol{\Lambda}\boldsymbol{\eta} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \quad 
\boldsymbol{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\Theta}),
$$

\noindent with unit factor loadings ($\lambda_j = 1$) and fixed error variances $\theta_{jj}$ (determined during calibration) to achieve reliabilities
$$
\rho_{y_j} = \frac{\lambda_j^2 \phi_{kk}}{\lambda_j^2 \phi_{kk} + \theta_{jj}} \approx \rho, \quad \rho \in \{0.4, 0.6, 0.8\}.
$$

\noindent Each latent variable was measured by three indicators. Measurement errors were sampled independently from univariate normal distributions.



{{< pagebreak >}}



# Convergence Issues and Outliers Statistics 


```{r}
#| label: conv-table
#| echo: false
#| output: asis
#| message: false
#| warning: false

# STUDY 1: convergence & outliers summary (APA Style - landscape with longtable)
create_apa_convergence_table_study1 <- function(results_df) {
  results_df %>%
    filter(Parameter == "eta1") %>%
    select(
      Condition,
      Method, 
      Model, 
      Distribution, 
      SampleSize, 
      Reliability,
      N_Converged_This_Method,
      Convergence_Rate_This_Method,
      N_After_Global_Convergence,
      N_Outliers_This_Method,
      Outlier_Rate_This_Method,
      N_After_Global_Outlier,
      N_Final
    ) %>%
    mutate(
      Distribution = case_when(
        Distribution == "nonnormal" ~ "right-skewed",
        TRUE ~ Distribution
      ),
      Convergence_Rate_This_Method = round(Convergence_Rate_This_Method, 1),
      Outlier_Rate_This_Method = round(Outlier_Rate_This_Method, 1)
    ) %>%
    arrange(Condition, factor(Model, levels = c("Full", "Linear")), Method)
}

# table
conv_table_study1 <- create_apa_convergence_table_study1(results_study_1)

tab_caption_study1 <- "Convergence and Outlier Statistics (Simulation 1)"
tab_note_study1 <- "N Conv. = number of replications that converged for this method. Conv. Rate = percentage of replications that converged (%). N Global Conv. = sample size after entire non-convergent iterations across approaches excluded. N Outliers = number of outliers detected for this method. Outlier Rate = percentage of converged cases flagged as outliers (%). N Global Outlier = sample size after global outlier removal across methods. N Final = final sample size after all exclusions."

kableExtra::kbl(
  conv_table_study1,
  booktabs = TRUE,
  longtable = TRUE,
  align = c("l", "l", "l", "l", "r", "r",
            "r", "r", "r",
            "r", "r",
            "r", "r"),
  col.names = c("Condition", "Method", "Model", "Distribution", "N", "Reliability",
                "N Conv.", "Conv. Rate", "N Global Conv.",
                "N Outliers", "Outlier Rate", "N Global Outlier", "N Final"),
  caption = tab_caption_study1,
  linesep = ""
) %>%
  kableExtra::add_header_above(c(
    " " = 6,
    "Convergence" = 3,
    "Outliers" = 2,
    "Final" = 2
  )) %>%
  kableExtra::kable_styling(
    latex_options = c("hold_position", "repeat_header", "scale_down"),
    font_size = 6,
    position = "left"
  ) %>%
  kableExtra::collapse_rows(columns = 1, valign = "top", latex_hline = "major") %>%
  kableExtra::footnote(
    general = tab_note_study1,
    general_title = "Note.",
    threeparttable = TRUE,
    escape = TRUE)
  #) %>%
  #kableExtra::landscape()


# STUDY 2: convergence & outliers summary (APA Style - landscape with longtable)
create_apa_convergence_table_study2 <- function(results_df, equation = "eta4") {
  param <- ifelse(equation == "eta4", "eta1", "eta4")
  
  results_df %>%
    filter(Equation == equation, Parameter == param) %>%
    select(
      Condition,
      Method, 
      Model, 
      Distribution, 
      SampleSize, 
      Reliability,
      N_Converged_This_Method,
      Convergence_Rate_This_Method,
      N_After_Global_Convergence,
      N_Outliers_This_Method,
      Outlier_Rate_This_Method,
      N_After_Global_Outlier,
      N_Final
    ) %>%
    mutate(
      Distribution = case_when(
        Distribution == "nonnormal" ~ "right-skewed",
        TRUE ~ Distribution
      ),
      Convergence_Rate_This_Method = round(Convergence_Rate_This_Method, 1),
      Outlier_Rate_This_Method = round(Outlier_Rate_This_Method, 1)
    ) %>%
    arrange(Condition, factor(Model, levels = c("Full", "Linear")), Method)
}


# STUDY 2 - EQUATION ETA4 TABLE (because it is general; eta5 would be the same)
conv_table_study2_eta4 <- create_apa_convergence_table_study2(results_study_2, "eta4")

tab_caption_study2_eta4 <- "Convergence and Outlier Statistics (Simulation 2)"
tab_note_study2 <- "N Conv. = number of replications that converged for this method. Conv. Rate = percentage of replications that converged (%). N Global Conv. = sample size after requiring all methods to converge. N Outliers = number of outliers detected for this method. Outlier Rate = percentage of converged cases flagged as outliers (%). N Global Outlier = sample size after global outlier removal across methods. N Final = final sample size after all exclusions."

kableExtra::kbl(
  conv_table_study2_eta4,
  booktabs = TRUE,
  longtable = TRUE,
  align = c("l", "l", "l", "l", "r", "r",
            "r", "r", "r",
            "r", "r",
            "r", "r"),
  col.names = c("Condition", "Method", "Model", "Distribution", "N", "Reliability",
                "N Conv.", "Conv. Rate", "N Global Conv.",
                "N Outliers", "Outlier Rate", "N Global Outlier", "N Final"),
  caption = tab_caption_study2_eta4,
  linesep = ""
) %>%
  kableExtra::add_header_above(c(
    " " = 6,
    "Convergence" = 3,
    "Outliers" = 2,
    "Final" = 2
  )) %>%
  kableExtra::kable_styling(
    latex_options = c("hold_position", "repeat_header", "scale_down"),
    font_size = 6,
    position = "left"
  ) %>%
  kableExtra::collapse_rows(columns = 1, valign = "top", latex_hline = "major") %>%
  kableExtra::footnote(
    general = tab_note_study2,
    general_title = "Note.",
    threeparttable = TRUE,
    escape = TRUE)
  #) %>%
  #kableExtra::landscape()
```

